#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9849b9-397b-5fb9-bb4b-01f3d013c5c2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "52fc2fee-c015-55e0-b411-03c86d5992e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5447" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown below. Find $\\triangle A'B'C',$ the image of $\\triangle ABC$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/4986bd2dab6b455c0d5e41909377c433d761bf7270364d3e2596e6db7f23981b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\triangle ABC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $\\triangle A'B'C'$ are $(-3,-4), (0,0), (1,-2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/e8a14ffbaab4597a2a4a7c9a6bad61dabbc4970fc22c3ed2eb28b2594a670938.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85936915-4c9c-572c-af99-025fec0f7570" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Line Segment Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "8b7758b4-e0ec-5872-90d7-1136c9716574" :question/math-academy-id "e-6682" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe line segment $\\overline{AB}$ is shown below. Find $\\overline{A'B'},$ the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/2e22400d15abc81150d6d18de591549e81a46bd16ab9e95f10f86bae2125b490.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overline{AB}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are $A'(1,3)$ and $B'(1,-2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/472f8e712f7d695d5ab3593dafbcc0309feb1a6ac80e9e29c4893dc651d20cf7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9849b9-397b-5fb9-bb4b-01f3d013c5c2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f322ad-95a5-5c05-b401-86954c9727d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/02b7a778b46085876e5269e0b088dde52293d5230cd0312459ec21c8b2600f71.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283468} {:answer/id #uuid "c784b9a6-3843-51dd-b0c0-04e05a66b725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/5485aedcf92aca355118c8bd051f16a1436d5f955a0dde65c85eaa0398eb03b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283469} {:answer/id #uuid "5ddaf679-ec51-59f2-8b9a-5528425d9db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/4c43c4c793924d4fde32b59c93b7a19be0109a389b126665868396b4c06d721e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283470} {:answer/id #uuid "a13f5005-67c7-58aa-ba32-b4b9e342f7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/77829a2aeb4fd7209a5ab5abfe2f84ab2225fc37f1a84fd25a4883647cff5fe1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283471} {:answer/id #uuid "869f085e-90c3-5d5b-8228-6ebeabbf0ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/98150e2e7b7cb55643f588b885c3f57a34da186834dcbebd2cb7b35151317519.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ddaf679-ec51-59f2-8b9a-5528425d9db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/4c43c4c793924d4fde32b59c93b7a19be0109a389b126665868396b4c06d721e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283470} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fdab775-4b09-5ed3-aac0-a666ef9ec146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b95133b0-1616-5846-9583-0eb3e1ac9ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-159801" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/d9ec98b03009d1023668aa12de88ed5515f0ea6df058d1b5d17804bf42082c5c.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown above. Which of the following is $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′},$ the image of $△ABC$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $T$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $△ABC:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $△{A}^{′}{B}^{′}{C}^{′}$ are $(6,4),(1,-6),(-3,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/4c43c4c793924d4fde32b59c93b7a19be0109a389b126665868396b4c06d721e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85936915-4c9c-572c-af99-025fec0f7570" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Line Segment Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d92617cf-f17a-583c-bfc8-1a5d816e5889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/dbb1063de834b81ffc00839234d5f8e782daa5f14d78b0a5f2d06265d48be07f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283520} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd02342-a287-52d7-98b1-5763069b10c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/0f67bd4698105bd6fc756db032dcb02160a1fed4afb1408d35a16b3f89d75b4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283521} {:answer/id #uuid "e324a1bd-e355-589a-88c9-8f6b97050565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/012ce15386906930741f9f369ccebf557b89001856738f0b646bfc6b083f6e57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283522} {:answer/id #uuid "5cec5545-919b-5b4d-ab6d-8af13dd70b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/be396df84a798da087bb7656433378c9ef6b4b6927482b24029a656ae6a85340.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283523} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce09fef-e69d-5f31-bbe3-cf0c90799be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/26d4f8e966818e27a0858da0aa8757cf71ebbd1ef0e0544a2b8a126810252def.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ce09fef-e69d-5f31-bbe3-cf0c90799be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/26d4f8e966818e27a0858da0aa8757cf71ebbd1ef0e0544a2b8a126810252def.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283524} :answer-field/id #uuid "93655f00-920b-521a-a23b-29ce5f50700d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1742b07-44a3-5a74-8a7a-20d14975ace1" :question/math-academy-id "q-77709" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/fa8ce497e80e941730cebb8528b09725930fcb31c839ac0020ce7cf9f0a39b58.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is shown above. Which of the following is $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}},$ the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(-3,6)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,-3),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13990291/assets/26d4f8e966818e27a0858da0aa8757cf71ebbd1ef0e0544a2b8a126810252def.png)"}]}
